{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe Gerrit Verified matrix intermittently fails\n`tests/unit/test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py::test_ledger_attribution_is_unchanged_by_the_batching`\nduring FIXTURE SETUP, at `git merge -q --no-edit side`. Observed on Python 3.12 in run\n31266912051 while validating a comment-only change; 11,434 other tests passed and the changed\nfile (`tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py`) cannot affect this fixture.\n\n## The original premise is REFUTED — this was never a content conflict\n\nThe ticket title and body assert \"a content conflict\". That was inferred from an exit code, not\nobserved, and it cannot be what happened.\n\nThe `tracker` fixture is built entirely from hard-coded literals: event filenames, contents (a\nfixed 4-key JSON dict), commit messages, and identity. There is **no timestamp source, no\n`uuid4`, no `rebar` store write, and no glob-order dependence** anywhere in it.\n`rebar._store.event_append.event_filename` is never reached. The event-filename-collision theory\nis refuted by construction.\n\nMerge shape:\n\n    base                 : {1000, 1001}\n    side                 : base + 1002, 1500-shared01, 1003\n    tickets (after reset): base + 1004, 1500-shared01, 1005\n\nExactly one path is touched by both sides — `…/1500-shared01-STATUS.json` — and both sides write\n**byte-identical** bytes. Verified in a scratch replica:\n\n    $ git rev-parse side:…/1500-shared01-STATUS.json  tickets:…/1500-shared01-STATUS.json\n    6bfe14090f6fd06399eb8160c875b3b62d4528dc\n    6bfe14090f6fd06399eb8160c875b3b62d4528dc\n    $ git merge-tree --write-tree tickets side ; echo rc=$?\n    8982dbe069e15adb85b51efbbfea6dd77e6d0c45\n    rc=0            # zero conflicts, structurally\n\nmerge-ort resolves a same-OID add/add trivially, and no path is deleted on either side, so\nrename detection has nothing to pair. Timing cannot change literal bytes.\n\n## Why the true cause is unknown: the fixture destroys the evidence\n\n`git merge` rc=1 has at least three distinct causes (probed on git 2.55): a genuine content\nconflict; a failing `pre-merge-commit` or `commit-msg` hook; and an unresolvable merge argument\n(`merge: side - not something we can merge`). Other failure classes exit 2 or 128.\n\nThe CI traceback contains only `CalledProcessError … returned non-zero exit status 1`. The\nfixture's `_git()` helper runs with `capture_output=True`, and `CalledProcessError.__str__`\ndoes **not** render `stdout`/`stderr` — so git's actual message was captured and then thrown\naway. Nothing in the record distinguishes the three causes.\n\nReproduction attempts found nothing: 1,500 replays of the exact git sequence (300 serial + 8x150\nconcurrent), 40 serial module runs, 72 module runs at 6-way process oversubscription, and 3 full\n`tests/unit` rounds under CI-parity `-n 4 --dist worksteal` (9,302 tests/round) — **zero\noccurrences**. Also refuted: non-isolated tmp dirs under xdist (each worker gets its own\n`popen-gwN` basetemp, and `repo.mkdir()` without `exist_ok` would raise on reuse), a leaked git\nshim or `GIT_*` env, and a concurrent session wiping the basetemp.\n\n## Scope of THIS change\n\nBecause the cause is unknown and the stated cause is impossible, no fixture rewrite is\nwarranted — that would be patching a mechanism nobody has observed. The one defect that IS\nproven is the destroyed evidence, so this change makes the failure diagnosable and nothing else.\n\n`_git()` will surface git's stdout and stderr, the failing argv, and the exit code when a git\ncommand fails. The exception TYPE stays `subprocess.CalledProcessError` so any existing\n`except` clause keeps working; only its rendered message gains the diagnostics.\n\nThis does not stop the flake. It guarantees that the next occurrence names its own cause instead\nof leaving a bare exit code, which is the prerequisite for ever fixing it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A failing `_git()` call surfaces git's stderr in the raised error's message.\n- [ ] A failing `_git()` call surfaces git's stdout in the raised error's message.\n- [ ] The raised error names the failing argv.\n- [ ] The raised error names the exit code.\n- [ ] The failure remains catchable as `subprocess.CalledProcessError`.\n- [ ] A regression test covers a failing `_git()` call and asserts the message carries git's\n      stderr; it fails before this change.\n- [ ] The fixture's merge history and its ledger-attribution oracle are unchanged.\n- [ ] No file under `src/rebar/` is modified by this change."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786505141349574001,"uuid":"745af3f2-a5f5-4aae-be3b-1ace88f012e8"}